        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MARK J. RICCIARDI, ESQ., Las Vegas, Nevada; on behalf of the Petitioner."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mark J. Ricciardi",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case involves the extraordinary situation in the law where the burden of proof is shifted to a defendant. Courts have recognized this type of burden shift in certain limited situations, certain torts, and in 1989 this Court recognized that burden shift in Title VII cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: And what is your definition of direct evidence, Mr. Ricciardi?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Irving L. Gornstein",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0000-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Since 1964, Title VII's disparate treatment prohibition has required a finding that a protected characteristic such as gender was a but for cause of an adverse employment decision. Now, the '91 amendments create a special rule of liability for mixed-motive cases where proof of but for cause is not required."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Irving L. Gornstein",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0000-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The text that says, even though other factors also motivated the practice, make clear that the amendment only applies to mixed-motive cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the key and most relevant and pertinent background principle here was that before the amendment, direct evidence was required to make out a mixed-motive case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Do you say that because that's the way a lot of court of appeals determined, or do you think that was a necessity by virtue of the split in the opinions on this Court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So when you put the two decisions together, Price Waterhouse and McDonnell Douglas, the rule that emerges is in -- to get into the Price Waterhouse box, where you get a shift in the burden of proof, you need direct evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It left it where it was, and where it was is in purely circumstantial evidence cases, under McDonnell Douglas, once the employer introduces a nondiscriminatory explanation, the plaintiff has to carry the burden of proof of showing pretext and but for cause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the parties at that point and at that juncture had agreed that this definitely was a 107(a) case and it would go to the jury as such."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Statute",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Reading 703(m), which is Title VII 200e-2(m) -- and that's found at the respondent's brief, page 9 -- the section specifically states, except as otherwise provided in this title, an unlawful employment practice is established when a complaining party demonstrates a race, color, religion, sex, or national origin was a motivating factor for any employment practice, even though other factors also motivated the practice."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Peccole",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0007",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the plaintiff wins at that point. If the plaintiff demonstrates sex is a motivating factor, at that point plaintiff wins. The affirmative defense doesn't take away the plaintiff's victory. It just limits the remedy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Peccole",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "they clarified Price Waterhouse from the standpoint that there was no heightened burden, no direct evidence burden, no substantial factor burden."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Peccole",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0001-0009",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "McDonnell Douglas survives on your reading in a case in which the defendant does not go forward with anything? The plaintiff puts in enough to make a prima facie case. Defendant sits mute. McDonnell Douglas controls the result there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unnamed)",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "QUESTION: Does it -- does it -- just for clarifying in my mind, does it matter or doesn't it matter whether you say (m) governs a separate set of cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Peccole",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think that one -- one other point I would like to make is that 703(m) and the way the Ninth Circuit has approached it, has made it simple and easy for the judges to handle, for the trial attorneys to handle, and for the jury to handle through instructions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2002-02-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9717679c0c4b7d134334283034feedb180e21ceea4822f034c26dcd822c7ebac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9717679c0c4b-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Thank you, Mr. Ricciardi. The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
